WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.700
Welcome to Now You See It

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.860
This video of a train was first screened to
a group of people in France in January of

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:20.410
1896. Legend has it that the viewers of the
film were so overwhelmed by what looked like

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.980
a train coming right at them that many hid
under their seats or ran to the back of the

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:27.590
room in fear of getting hit. The truth of
this story is debated, but what’s certain

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:31.180
is that seeing a video for the first time
of a train coming towards you would probably

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.980
leave you pretty shocked and amazed. So the
question is, even more than a century later,

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.870
can we be just as moved by film, or are we
desensitized because we know the things on

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:41.130
screen aren’t actually happening to us

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.020
I want to explore the war film because war

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.149
films have such great potential to shock and amaze
us through their sheer scope and powerful

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:55.800
subject matter. How can films portray war,
and what techniques do filmmakers use to depict

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.240
war and the soldiers that fight them? Can
film portray war realistically?

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.040
Let's take a look at two types of war films: Pro-War and Anti-War

00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:08.080
French Filmmaker Francois Truffaut is often
attributed with saying “there’s no such

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.100
thing as an anti-war film” while Steven
Spielberg said in an interview with Newsweek

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.399
magazine “Of course every war movie, good
or bad, is an anti-war movie.” So, who’s

00:01:16.399 --> 00:01:19.969
right? I want to argue that while neither
of these statements is correct, they do give

00:01:19.969 --> 00:01:24.540
insight into the nature of film. But before
arguing if a film is pro or anti-war, we have

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.420
to define pro and anti-war.
A definition like “anti-war portrays war

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.250
in a bad light and pro-war portrays war in
a good light” is somewhat of an oversimplification.

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:38.259
A lot of war films show a combination of positive
and negative aspects of war. They Were Expendable,

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:43.009
for example, shows Japanese airplanes blowing
up a US PT boat and killing a soldier. Look at

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:47.390
this shot of John Wayne collapsing at the
sight of the boat, weighed down by his combat boots

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:51.600
the camera following him to the ground. This scene
reflects war as cruel, dangerous, and deadly.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.359
But if we look at the movie as a whole, it’s
definitely a pro-war propaganda film.

00:01:55.359 --> 00:01:58.280
Look at this shot of General MacArthur, commander
of the Army. He towers

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.760
over the camera, speaks little, and is treated
by the soldiers as a celebrity.

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.160
"Sir, please autograph my hat."

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.970
He's looked at as a hero and savior for the United States, and this,

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.280
and along with lots of pro-war sentiment in the film,

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:16.420
shows that They Were Expendable is much more pro-war than anti-war. I think a better definition for a

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.620
pro-war film would be a film that shows the
benefits of war outweighing the costs, and an

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:25.480
anti-war film shows the costs of war outweighing the benefits. A modern example of a pro-war film is

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.519
American Sniper. Clint Eastwood makes the claim that
the film is anti-war, and it definitely has

00:02:29.519 --> 00:02:33.390
a lot of aspects of an anti-war film. The
film shows that war isn’t always black and

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.640
white and can cause serious emotional trauma.
But what makes it pro-war is that Chris Kyle

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:39.620
believes he did the right thing.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:42.880
" I was just protecting my guys, they were trying to kill our soldiers and

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.720
I'm willing to meet my creator and answer for every shot that I took"

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.300
The film is pro-war because it conveys that, despite

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.770
all the trauma of war, war seems worth it.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:56.970
"And then there are those who have been blessed with the gift of aggression

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.220
and the overpowering need to protect the flock.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.520
These men are the rare breed who live to confront the wolf.

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.560
They are the sheepdog."

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.320
The benefits of war outweigh the cost, and war is a necessarily evil against those who cause terror.

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.800
It's with films like American Sniper

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:17.410
where I think Spielberg is wrong in his statement about war films.

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:20.460
War films normally don't make out war as something to look forward to

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.780
but they can certainly make war out as necessary.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.560
Pro-war films use heroes and an idea of the greater good

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.860
to convey the message that war is necessary

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.600
and anti-war films showcase the brutality and cruelty

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.700
of war to portray combat negatively

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.280
To get a look at how filmmakers use cinematography

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.200
to have an anti-war film show the horrors and realities of combat

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.960
one has to look no further than the D-Day scene in

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.800
Saving Private Ryan.

00:03:47.769 --> 00:03:51.500
The transition from the old man in the opening
scene to the beaches of Normandy is done with

00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:55.970
a jarring smash cut. We don’t fade in, we
don’t zoom in. It’s a fast and abrupt

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:59.709
transition. It gives the effect of being forced
into the scene without adequate preparation.

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:04.120
The shot of the endless rows of anti-landing
barriers on the beach looks eerily similar

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.409
to the rows of tombstones in the graveyard
we just saw in the earlier scene. A foreshadowing

00:04:08.409 --> 00:04:12.099
of what’s to come. We get several close
ups of soldiers, and they certainly aren’t

00:04:12.099 --> 00:04:16.959
the typical war heroes. The first image of
our hero is his hand shaking, a sign of fear.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:20.620
Compare that to the grand introduction of
John Wayne on a squadron of PT Boats that

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:24.780
are basically showing off to the camera. Or even American Sniper where Chris Kyle first gets shown

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.430
with his huge powerful sniper rifle. The Omaha
beach scene shows a war fought by the terrified,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.940
not by the heroic or masculine.
The killing on the beach is best described

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:37.770
as pure chaos. The camera shake, point of
view shots and minimal movement blur all work

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:42.020
together to pull the viewer onto the battlefield
alongside the other soldiers. With all these

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:46.440
effects, this scene definitely conveys that
the costs of war outweigh the benefits. A

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:50.590
great way for a film to portray war in a negative
way is to make war scenes as realistic as

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.790
possible. By putting us in the real world, we feel the terror

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.509
and get a sense that war may not be worth all the turmoil it causes

00:04:57.509 --> 00:05:01.910
Waltz with Bashir is another great example
of pulling us into the action and showing

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:06.530
how was can have such devastating effects. The movie is an animation war film, and the resulting effect

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.639
is that we're removed from the reality in the film

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:13.100
because it’s animated and doesn’t
look realistic. Then, in the last minute or so

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:17.550
the film quickly cuts to actual footage of the aftermath
of the massacres in Lebanon. The result is

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:22.150
heart wrenching. It jars us from the spectacle
of the animation into the harshness of reality.

00:05:22.150 --> 00:05:27.250
We see widows crying, dead bodies, and it’s
all thrown at us with no preparation for the transition

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.970
from cartoon to reality. The effect is extremely powerful, and we realize

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:33.660
that what we were originally seeing only in animated characters

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.020
is in fact exactly the reality of war

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:38.120
and that reality is horrific suffering

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.280
If that’s not an anti-war film, then I don’t know

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.949
what is.
I do see where Truffaut is coming from when

00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:47.410
he says there is no such thing as an anti-war
film. He’s saying that film by nature contains

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.780
spectacle. Since we’re not actually on the
battlefield and are safe in our movie

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.720
theater seats, we don’t completely feel
exactly like the soldiers do because we’re

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:58.479
removed from the reality. This removal makes
it difficult to construct a film that truly

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:02.240
leaves people disgusted at war because we’re
safe from it. We can enjoy watching the destruction

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.740
of film in the theater. Famous director Samuel Fuller, upon watching the very anti-war

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:10.260
Full Metal Jacket, stated that it was "just another god damn recruiting film."

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:14.660
Even when films try to criticize war, sometimes they can still give the effect of making war glamorous

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.820
Cinema as an art form is limited because no matter how drawn in

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:22.030
we are to the movie, there’s always that barrier
from reality. A train on screen doesn't

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:26.080
frighten us like it would in real life.
So maybe we are a little desensitized by the

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.500
screen. We aren’t shocked like the viewers
of the train were back in 1896. Some may say

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:34.220
that’s a limitation to film. But to others
it’s a challenge. It’s a challenge all

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:39.060
filmmakers have to tackle. They have to surprise
and astonish us even when we're used to watching film

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.660
and maybe not every blockbuster

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.580
these days can blow you away, but every once
in a while, if you’re lucky, you get to

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:46.860
see a film that hits you like a freight train.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.530
Thanks for watching.

